Microsoft Learn: Configure Azure App Service Plans

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Configure Azure App Service Plans
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to configure Azure App Service Plans, select the appropriate pricing tier, implement scaling strategies, and configure autoscaling for web applications hosted in Azure App Service.

This module focused on understanding how App Service Plans provide the compute resources behind Azure Web Apps, how pricing tiers affect features and performance, and how to scale applications efficiently to meet changing workload demands.

Step 1: Understanding Azure App Service Plans

The module introduced Azure App Service Plans as the compute infrastructure that hosts Azure App Service applications.

An App Service Plan defines:

CPU resources
Memory allocation
Storage resources
Scaling capabilities
Geographic region
Relationship:
App Service Plan
       ↓
Web App
Web App
API App
Web App

Multiple applications can run inside the same App Service Plan and share its resources.

Benefits:
Simplified application hosting
Centralized resource management
Easy scaling
Cost efficiency

The module emphasized that App Service Plans provide the underlying infrastructure for Azure App Services.

Step 2: Understanding App Service Plan Components

The training covered the core characteristics of an App Service Plan.

Plan characteristics:
Component	Purpose
Region	Deployment location
Operating System	Windows or Linux
Pricing Tier	Determines features and capacity
Compute Resources	CPU and memory allocation
Scaling Limits	Number of instances supported
Key concepts learned:
Applications inherit the capabilities of their App Service Plan
Multiple apps can share one plan
Resource usage is shared among applications

The module highlighted proper planning when deploying App Service workloads.

Step 3: Understanding Azure App Service Pricing Tiers

A major focus of the module was selecting the appropriate pricing tier.

Azure App Service offers multiple pricing options.

Common tiers:
Tier	Purpose
Free (F1)	Development and testing
Shared (D1)	Low-cost testing
Basic (B1-B3)	Small production workloads
Standard (S1-S3)	Production applications
Premium (P1-P5)	High-performance workloads
Isolated	Enterprise workloads
Key lessons learned:
Higher tiers provide greater performance
Higher tiers support additional features
Pricing affects scalability and availability
Step 4: Comparing Pricing Tier Features

The training compared capabilities across tiers.

Feature Comparison
Feature	Free	Basic	Standard	Premium
Custom Domains	No	Yes	Yes	Yes
SSL Support	Limited	Yes	Yes	Yes
Autoscaling	No	No	Yes	Yes
Deployment Slots	No	No	Yes	Yes
Daily Backup	No	No	Yes	Yes
Key concepts learned:
Production environments typically require Standard or Premium plans
Free and Shared tiers are primarily used for development and testing

Understanding tier capabilities is frequently tested on AZ-104.

Step 5: Understanding Scale Up (Vertical Scaling)

The module introduced Scale Up.

Scale Up increases the resources available to an App Service Plan by moving to a larger pricing tier.

Example:

Before:

Basic B1
1 CPU
1.75 GB RAM

After:

Standard S1
More CPU
More RAM
Benefits:
Increased performance
More memory
Better processing power
Key lessons learned:
Scale Up changes the size of the infrastructure
Existing applications remain in place
Suitable for resource-intensive workloads
Step 6: Understanding Scale Out (Horizontal Scaling)

The training introduced Scale Out.

Scale Out increases the number of application instances.

Example:

Before:

1 Instance

After:

4 Instances
Traffic Flow:
Users
   ↓
Load Balancer
 ↓   ↓   ↓   ↓
App App App App
Benefits:
Increased capacity
Improved availability
Better fault tolerance

The module emphasized that Scale Out is generally preferred for cloud-native applications.

Step 7: Comparing Scale Up and Scale Out

The module compared both scaling approaches.

Scaling Method	Description
Scale Up	Increase VM size
Scale Out	Increase VM count
Example:

Scale Up:

Small Server
      ↓
Large Server

Scale Out:

1 Server
      ↓
5 Servers
Key lessons learned:
Scale Up improves resource capacity
Scale Out improves scalability and resiliency
Many applications benefit from a combination of both
Step 8: Understanding Autoscale

A major focus of the module was Autoscale.

Autoscale automatically adjusts application resources based on workload demand.

Benefits:
Improved performance
Reduced administrative effort
Cost optimization
Automatic resource management
Example:

Low traffic:

2 Instances

High traffic:

10 Instances

The module demonstrated how Azure automatically reacts to workload changes.

Step 9: Configuring Autoscale Rules

The training covered autoscale configuration.

Autoscale can be triggered by metrics.

Common metrics:
Metric	Example
CPU Utilization	Above 70%
Memory Usage	High demand
HTTP Queue Length	Increased requests
Schedule-Based Rules	Business hours scaling
Example Rule:
If CPU > 70%
Add 2 Instances
Scale-In Rule:
If CPU < 30%
Remove 1 Instance
Benefits:
Automatic scaling
Cost control
Improved user experience
Step 10: Understanding Autoscale Profiles

The module introduced Autoscale Profiles.

Profiles allow different scaling behaviors based on:

Time of day
Day of week
Business schedules
Performance thresholds
Example:

Business Hours:

Minimum: 4 Instances
Maximum: 10 Instances

After Hours:

Minimum: 1 Instance
Maximum: 3 Instances
Benefits:
Predictable scaling
Lower operating costs
Better resource utilization
